# @mail between two players in different rooms. Runs last: the mail it sends stays in the mailboxes.
::case mail.fwd-by-name  @mail/fwd matches recipients by player name, not by what is nearby
::login alice Alice alicepass
::login bob Bob bobpass
::as bob
home
::as alice
@mail Alice=Forward me/A note to pass on.
@mail/fwd 1=Bob
@mail/fwd 1=Nobody
@mail/fwd 1=Bob Alice
::as bob
@tel me=[loc(*Alice)]
::as alice

::case mail.fwd-refused  a forward the recipient's mail lock refuses is silent and still counted
::as bob
@lock/mail me=!*Alice
::as alice
@mail/fwd 1=*Bob
::as bob
@unlock/mail me

::case mail.functions-own-mailbox  mail()-family functions let a mortal read their own mailbox, not another's
::as alice
think mail(me)
think mail(*Alice)
think mailsubject(me,1)
think mail(*Bob)
think mail(*Bob,1)
think mailsubject(*Bob,1)

::case mail.fetch-failures  what the mail fetch functions return for a message or player they cannot find
::as alice
think mail(99)
think mail(nosuchmailplayer)
think mail(me,99)
think mail(*nosuchmailplayer,1)
think mail(me,abc)
think mailsubject(99)
think mailsubject(me,99)
think mailsubject(*nosuchmailplayer,1)
think mailsubject(me,abc)
think mailfrom(99)
think mailstatus(99)
think mailtime(99)
think mailsubject(1)
think mailfrom(1)
think mailstatus(1)
think mailtime(1)
think mail(here)
think mail(here,1)
think mailfrom(here,1)
think maillist(here,1)
think maillist(*nosuchmailplayer,1)

::case mail.malias-lookup  @malias members: lookup_player tests # before it drops one * (#1391)
::as bob
@malias +parity1391=*Alice *#1 **Alice
